#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded By a Curve When It Crosses the X-Axis Several Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "997fa14e-e274-5fe7-be4b-5610a116c67f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293258} {:answer/id #uuid "f06d164d-719f-5c1c-8cf0-a0522d529f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293259} {:answer/id #uuid "718aa89a-1e3c-50fc-8d11-8b0fd1e910ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293260} {:answer/id #uuid "0b42f7d7-6be9-59c0-9c20-09c868147bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293261} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa61a86-fa52-5f24-861c-2f11860db70b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b42f7d7-6be9-59c0-9c20-09c868147bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293261} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b954d8-703b-5f17-861d-01c5d31c157b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0f8da35-3e73-511d-863e-fb9605a16342" :question/math-academy-id "q-140573" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/27bb4ee622f0b80e4b1a6edfae2c42f0676cafd0f9ad6150b979b32dbdabfc62.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2-x)(2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 2$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-2,2],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-2,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5db9528f-2cd5-59dd-80e0-1ea3fe0cb9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293265} {:answer/id #uuid "840d1d60-7290-5b0c-a41c-6d0f08271d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293266} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2f8fdb-50fb-53b1-90f2-65dde0305b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293267} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a28015-5437-5ba5-83cd-8e31a8e550da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293268} {:answer/id #uuid "49a0a506-a2f3-51dc-ab33-e3ab024ca168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2f8fdb-50fb-53b1-90f2-65dde0305b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293267} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b62923-b86b-50a8-b103-185ef2ff691f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20edd780-8f94-530f-99ac-d687f3a1c198" :question/math-academy-id "q-54476" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=4{x}^{3}+4$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is our only real solution. Since $-1\\in [-2,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/b0f2899bf6a946f6d7bf8bc60c41827bd05aef500f0fc0f30a5916c26b1007bb.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-4x{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[(-1)^{4}-(-2)^{4}]-4[-1+2] \\\\  & =-(-15)-4 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,1]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+4x{|}_{-1}^{-1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{4}-(-1)^{4}]+4[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =0+8 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=11+8=19.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded By a Curve When It Crosses the X-Axis Several Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7572b8c-1187-5fd6-820c-c93b71b3ad8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{0}(x^{3} - x)dx + ∫_{0}^{1}(x^{3} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109590} {:answer/id #uuid "b8507b97-66a1-5429-853e-374f0ed9f176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1} - (x^{3} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109591} {:answer/id #uuid "db3003d0-3807-58e8-9190-449768a3f554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{0}(x^{3} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109592} {:answer/id #uuid "37fe8bb9-1d8a-52f0-a2c5-564cc6460ced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{0}(x^{3} - x)dx + ∫_{0}^{1} - (x^{3} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109593} {:answer/id #uuid "6d15ebb2-37cb-5c5f-b792-57a7e905870c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1}(x^{3} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37fe8bb9-1d8a-52f0-a2c5-564cc6460ced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{0}(x^{3} - x)dx + ∫_{0}^{1} - (x^{3} - x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109593} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2cbe6b7-5888-5fbb-989b-b8fdd5b2749f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a5ec6e8-06a7-53a9-aedc-bfb4a4f7d32a" :question/math-academy-id "q-54482" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/a8a85024d555378ad7ba31262a290386777fa94b00208d6a68fdacb5bb61d3c8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}-x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 1$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-1,1],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cc4423-b6ad-5fc1-849c-834d7bb55dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293272} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4dd8b7-da23-517c-8bc9-e8c8becfe79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293273} {:answer/id #uuid "09b83084-8f76-5b51-809d-e3f93f85d22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293274} {:answer/id #uuid "1296e85a-a8af-5729-a573-7b7fade82f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293275} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1e25de-f003-59ac-bea2-86bbc646c702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b83084-8f76-5b51-809d-e3f93f85d22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293274} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5cf1c0c-1eb5-564b-abf3-2c8a22d0c79d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa58bcd-5c50-5bca-9be6-138888c6899f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54494" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/47e15993f3957fb3c1d7619fcf62544b2d5b358715538e17b9232dec5a0172c7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=0$ and $x=2$ is $x=1.$\n\nSince $1\\in [0,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,2]$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =([\\frac{32}{5}-2]-[\\frac{1}{5}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{26}{5}=\\frac{30}{5}=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded By a Curve When It Crosses the X-Axis Several Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ace5023f-8965-5310-a3c6-6b636c1b0cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109602} {:answer/id #uuid "d2effc5b-233a-54a1-94e8-31a57dffa7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109603} {:answer/id #uuid "341cf3df-8724-5eff-b2af-33fa7a0bfdf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109604} {:answer/id #uuid "34d6155a-6f4e-5ed0-8f64-c5d78df72838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109605} {:answer/id #uuid "470fe8b5-fec6-5a1b-9b93-0f00fbe7aad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2effc5b-233a-54a1-94e8-31a57dffa7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109603} :answer-field/id #uuid "edb8e90e-0136-5c9f-b654-0a469af4d382" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75ff9fb5-0c01-5b20-bd14-b3bcbfcd02fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-54513" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3x+3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x(1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ and $x=0$ are our real solutions. Since $-1\\in [-2,0]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030836/assets/16a25fdc08ed6db39932508fd829dc5f855b38ff9ec85083a08d05fed0d985ab.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,-1]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3x+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}x\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{-2}^{-1}+{x}^{3}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(-1)^{2}-(-2)^{2}]+[(-1)^{3}-(-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+7 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-1,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3x+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3x+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{-1}^{0}-{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[0-(-1)^{2}]-[0-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=3.\n$$"}]}
